            "is_markdown": false,
            "creator": "kevinushey",
            "time": "2026-11-03T19:25:26Z",
            "attachment_id": {},
            "count": 0,
            "bug_id": 19280,
            "text": "In sm_split3() (src/library/stats/src/smooth.c) the flag recording whether the S step changed anything is assigned per plateau side (chg = y[i] != x[i]) instead of OR-ed, so a later plateau whose split leaves the value unchanged resets it. smooth(x, \"S\") then reports \"NOT changed\" while altering the data, and sm_3RS3R()/sm_3RSS() run the S step and the following 3R only if(chg), returning the plain 3R result. This contradicts ?smooth (\"3RS3R is a concatenation of 3R, S and 3R, 3RSS similarly\") and the all.equal() assertions in its examples.\n\nReproducible example: attached as smooth-split-chg-repro.R; smooth(x) returns x unchanged (0.5 1 3 3 1 0) where 3R, S, 3R in turn gives 0.5 1 2 2 1 0. The reset needs exact ties, so continuous data is unaffected; on small-integer data about 5% of random series differ.\n\nAnalysis: both assignments date from the 2000 implementation; the if(chg) guard only means to skip the final 3R when S changed nothing (\"else y == z already\"). In 400 random integer series, smooth(x, \"S\") returned values different from its input while reporting \"changed\" = FALSE 14 times, and never the reverse. Seen on R-devel r90833.\n\nProposed patch: attached as smooth-split-chg.diff. It accumulates the flag on both sides and adds a regression test; 2000 random integer series then all agree with the composition, and reg-tests pass.\n\nThe patch is also submitted as https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/454, where CI results can be seen.",
            "creation_time": "2026-11-03T19:25:26Z",
            "is_private": false,
            "id": 104078,
            "tags": []
          },
